WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.360
Next, let's talk about allergies.

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:09.640
Think of an allergy as your immune system overreacting to something it sees as a threat,

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:12.880
like nuts, shellfish, or bee stings.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:16.840
Most reactions are mild, maybe some itchy eyes or hives.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:21.040
But if someone's airway starts to swell and they're struggling to breathe, you're looking

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.320
at a severe emergency called anaphylaxis.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.280
If that happens, call 911 right away and use an EpiPen.

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:31.520
It's important to know that the EpiPen isn't a cure.

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:36.060
It's a temporary boost of adrenaline that buys them time to breathe until help arrives.

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:40.799
To use it correctly under stress, just remember the rhyme, blue to the sky, orange to the

00:00:40.799 --> 00:00:41.799
thigh.

00:00:41.799 --> 00:00:46.279
You'll pull off the blue safety cap, press the orange tip firmly into their outer mid-thigh

00:00:46.279 --> 00:00:49.959
until it clicks, and hold it there for three to ten seconds.

00:00:49.959 --> 00:00:54.160
Your instructor will pass out some trainer EpiPens and demonstrate how to use them for

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:55.720
some hands-on experience.

